<div class="article-body">
    <Fragment set:html={`<h2>The Dead Hang Progression Ladder</h2>
        <p>A dead hang progression ladder moves from assisted hangs to one-arm holds in six levels. Each level builds on the grip endurance and shoulder stability of the one before it. The hold times below are practical targets, not norms. The doses are common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>

        <div class="visual-block">
          <div class="dg-box" role="group" aria-label="Six-level dead hang progression ladder from assisted dead hang to one-arm dead hang, each level with its target hold time and typical weeks">
<div class="dg-box-head"><span>Progression ladder</span><span>Target hold</span></div>
<div class="dg-ladder-wrap"><span class="dg-end dg-end-top" aria-hidden="true">Advanced</span><ol class="dg-ladder"><li style="--a:0.28"><span class="dg-lv">6</span><span class="dg-lname"><strong>One-Arm Dead Hang</strong><small>Weeks 10-12+</small></span><span class="dg-lval">5-15 seconds</span></li><li style="--a:0.24"><span class="dg-lv">5</span><span class="dg-lname"><strong>Weighted Dead Hang</strong><small>Weeks 8-12</small></span><span class="dg-lval">15-30 seconds</span></li><li style="--a:0.20"><span class="dg-lv">4</span><span class="dg-lname"><strong>Active Hang</strong><small>Weeks 6-10</small></span><span class="dg-lval">20-45 seconds</span></li><li style="--a:0.15"><span class="dg-lv">3</span><span class="dg-lname"><strong>Extended Dead Hang</strong><small>Weeks 4-8</small></span><span class="dg-lval">30-60 seconds</span></li><li style="--a:0.10"><span class="dg-lv">2</span><span class="dg-lname"><strong>Basic Dead Hang</strong><small>Weeks 2-4</small></span><span class="dg-lval">15-30 seconds</span></li><li style="--a:0.06"><span class="dg-lv">1</span><span class="dg-lname"><strong>Assisted Dead Hang</strong><small>Weeks 1-2</small></span><span class="dg-lval">15 seconds</span></li></ol><span class="dg-end dg-end-bottom" aria-hidden="true">Beginner</span></div>
</div>
          <p class="caption">Progression path from assisted hangs to one-arm dead hangs.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Progression Overview</h3>
          <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Level</th><th scope="col">Exercise</th><th scope="col">Target Hold</th><th scope="col">Common Timeline</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>1</td><td>Assisted Dead Hang</td><td>15 seconds</td><td>Weeks 1-2</td></tr>
              <tr><td>2</td><td>Basic Dead Hang</td><td>15-30 seconds</td><td>Weeks 2-4</td></tr>
              <tr><td>3</td><td>Extended Dead Hang</td><td>30-60 seconds</td><td>Weeks 4-8</td></tr>
              <tr><td>4</td><td>Active Hang</td><td>20-45 seconds</td><td>Weeks 6-10</td></tr>
              <tr><td>5</td><td>Weighted Dead Hang</td><td>15-30 seconds</td><td>Weeks 8-12</td></tr>
              <tr><td>6</td><td>One-Arm Dead Hang</td><td>5-15 seconds</td><td>Weeks 10-12+</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
        </div>

        <p>Timelines vary with bodyweight and training history. Heavier people often take longer at each level. Climbers and manual laborers may skip the first levels. Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days and add a session only if you stay pain free, as the <a href="/deadhang-frequency/">dead hang frequency guide</a> explains.</p>

        <h2>Level 1: Assisted Dead Hang</h2>
        <p>The assisted dead hang reduces bodyweight load on your grip. Use this level if you cannot yet hold a standard dead hang for 15 seconds. Foot support and a loop band are the two common assistance methods.</p>

        <h3>Feet-on-Ground Method</h3>
        <p>Place a box or bench beneath the bar. Grab the bar and hang with your toes touching the surface. Foot support takes part of your weight. Reduce foot contact over time to put more of your weight on the bar.</p>

        <h3>Band-Assisted Method</h3>
        <p>Loop a resistance band over the bar. Step both feet into the band. The band takes part of your weight, and thicker bands take more. Record the assist you used so tests stay comparable. Move to thinner bands as your grip strengthens.</p>

        <h3>Level 1 Programming</h3>
        <p>Level 1 uses 3 sets of 15-second holds with as much assistance as you need to reach 15 seconds. Rest 90 seconds between sets. Move to Level 2 when you can hold 15 seconds across all 3 sets with minimal foot or band assistance.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/beginner-deadhang/">beginner dead hang guide</a> covers Level 1 assistance and setup in detail. A bent-arm option is the <a href="/flexed-arm-hang/">flexed arm hang</a>, a different hold from a dead hang.</p>

        <div class="visual-block">
          <div class="dg-box"><div class="dg-box-head"><span>Beginner weekly training split</span></div>
<table class="dg-week"><caption class="dg-sr">Sample beginner week: dead hang 3 sets of 15 seconds on Monday, Wednesday and Friday, with rest on the other four days</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Day</th><th scope="col">Prescription</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="dg-on"><th scope="row">Mon</th><td>Dead hang, 3 sets x 15 seconds</td></tr><tr class="dg-off"><th scope="row">Tue</th><td>Rest</td></tr><tr class="dg-on"><th scope="row">Wed</th><td>Dead hang, 3 sets x 15 seconds</td></tr><tr class="dg-off"><th scope="row">Thu</th><td>Rest</td></tr><tr class="dg-on"><th scope="row">Fri</th><td>Dead hang, 3 sets x 15 seconds</td></tr><tr class="dg-off"><th scope="row">Sat</th><td>Rest</td></tr><tr class="dg-off"><th scope="row">Sun</th><td>Rest</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
          <p class="caption">Sample beginner week: three hang days with rest days between. Add about 5 seconds to your target hold once you complete every set cleanly, roughly once a week.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Level 2: Basic Dead Hang</h2>
        <p>The basic dead hang uses your full bodyweight with no assistance. Grab the bar with an overhand grip at shoulder width. Step off the platform and hang with your arms fully extended and your shoulders relaxed in a passive position.</p>

        <h3>Level 2 Programming</h3>
        <p>Level 2 uses 3 sets of 15 to 30 second holds with 60 to 90 seconds of rest. Add about 5 seconds to your target hold once you complete every set cleanly, roughly once a week.</p>
        <p>Focus on <a href="/deadhang-form/">proper dead hang form</a> at this level. Keep your arms straight, shoulders relaxed, pelvis tucked and breathing steady. Form habits built at this level carry into every later level.</p>

        <h3>Progression Criteria</h3>
        <p>Move to Level 3 when you can hold 30 seconds for 3 sets with clean form. Your forearms may feel tired. Your form should not break down: no swinging, no bent elbows, no breath holding. Stop a second or two before your grip gives out and step down under control.</p>

        <h2>Level 3: Extended Dead Hang</h2>
        <p>Extended hangs push your grip endurance beyond 30 seconds. At Level 3 the challenge shifts from basic strength to sustained effort because your forearms must resist fatigue for 30 to 60 seconds under constant load.</p>

        <h3>Level 3 Programming</h3>
        <p>Level 3 uses 3 or 4 sets of 30 to 60 second holds with 60 to 90 seconds of rest. Mental focus becomes a training variable at this duration.</p>
        <p>Steady breathing helps you stay relaxed during a long hold. Holding your breath tends to shorten it. The breathing section of the <a href="/how-to-deadhang/">dead hang how-to guide</a> covers the cues.</p>

        <h3>Progression Criteria</h3>
        <p>Move to Level 4 when you hold 60 seconds for 3 sets. A 60-second hold is a personal milestone on this ladder. It is not a published standard and grip needs for climbing and lifting vary by task.</p>

        <h2>Level 4: Active Hang</h2>
        <p>The active hang adds scapular engagement to the dead hang. Pull your shoulder blades down and together while keeping your arms straight. Your shoulders move down a few centimeters from the passive position. Your lats and lower traps work to hold them there.</p>
        <p>Active hangs build the bottom-position strength used at the start of a <a href="/dead-hang-pull-up/">dead hang pull-up</a>. Scapular control at full arm extension is one of several things that limit new pull-up trainees. The active hang practices it.</p>

        <h3>Level 4 Programming</h3>
        <p>Level 4 uses 3 or 4 sets of 20 to 45 second holds with 60 to 90 seconds of rest. Alternate active and passive hangs across sessions.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/passive-vs-active-hang/">passive vs active hang guide</a> covers technique cues for both positions.</p>

        <h3>Progression Criteria</h3>
        <p>Move to Level 5 when you hold an active hang for 45 seconds across 3 sets. Your shoulder blade position should stay stable throughout the hold with no winging or upward drift in the final 10 seconds. Level 5 also needs a comfortable and pain-free bodyweight hang of about 45 seconds before you add load. This is a conservative guide, not a tested threshold.</p>

        <h2>Level 5: Weighted Dead Hang</h2>
        <p>Weighted dead hangs add external load to the bodyweight hang. Use a dip belt with weight plates or hold a dumbbell between your feet. Start with 5 to 10 lbs (2 to 5 kg) and add about 5 lbs once every set is clean.</p>
        <p>Added load makes bodyweight hangs hard again once they have become easy because your forearm flexors have to work against more than your bodyweight.</p>

        <h3>Level 5 Programming</h3>
        <p>Level 5 uses 3 or 4 sets of 15 to 30 second holds with 90 to 120 seconds of rest. Add weight only when every set is clean at the current load.</p>
        <p>Progress in small steps and record the load you used. The <a href="/weighted-hang-guide/">weighted dead hang guide</a> covers loading and equipment.</p>

        <h3>Progression Criteria</h3>
        <p>Move to Level 6 when you can hold a controlled two-arm hang for about 60 seconds. This is a conservative guide, not a tested threshold. Your grip and shoulders must also be pain free under load.</p>

        <h2>Level 6: One-Arm Dead Hang</h2>
        <p>The one-arm dead hang puts your entire bodyweight through one forearm, one shoulder and one side of your core. That is about twice the load each arm carries in a two-arm hang. It is an advanced exercise that needs careful preparation.</p>

        <h3>Assisted One-Arm Hang</h3>
        <p>Grab the bar with one hand. Wrap 2-3 fingers of your other hand around the wrist of your hanging arm. The assisting fingers take part of the load, by an amount that depends on how hard they press. Hold for 5 to 10 seconds per side.</p>

        <h3>Full One-Arm Hang</h3>
        <p>Grab the bar with one hand. Let your opposite arm hang at your side. Your body will turn slightly toward the hanging arm. Engage your core to resist excessive rotation. Hold for 5 to 15 seconds per arm.</p>

        <h3>Level 6 Programming</h3>
        <p>Level 6 uses 2 or 3 sets per arm with 2 to 3 minutes of rest. Alternate which arm goes first each session. The <a href="/one-arm-deadhang/">one-arm dead hang guide</a> has the full build-up.</p>

        <h2>When to Progress to the Next Level</h2>
        <p>Three criteria decide when to move up a level on this ladder, and all three must be met.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Progression Checklist</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Hold time met:</strong> You reach the target hold time for 3 consecutive sets in a single session.</li>
            <li><strong>Form quality maintained:</strong> No breakdown in arm extension, shoulder position, core engagement or breathing during any set.</li>
            <li><strong>No pain present:</strong> Zero sharp, shooting or radiating pain in your hands, wrists, elbows or shoulders during or after training.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <p>Meeting hold time alone is not sufficient. Hitting 30 seconds with bent elbows and erratic breathing does not count. Record yourself or train with a partner to check form at each level.</p>
        <p>If any pain develops, stay at your current level or drop back one level. The <a href="/injury-prevention/">injury prevention guide</a> covers elbow, shoulder and finger pain. A clinician can assess pain that persists.</p>

        <h2>Sample 12-Week Progression Plan</h2>
        <p>This 12-week table is a sample of how the six levels can be spaced for someone starting from zero. It is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Weeks</th><th scope="col">Level</th><th scope="col">Focus</th><th scope="col">Sets x Hold</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>1-2</td><td>Level 1</td><td>Assisted hangs, grip adaptation</td><td>3 x 15s</td></tr>
              <tr><td>3-4</td><td>Level 2</td><td>Full bodyweight hangs</td><td>3 x 15-25s</td></tr>
              <tr><td>5-6</td><td>Level 3</td><td>Extended holds, breathing focus</td><td>3 x 30-45s</td></tr>
              <tr><td>7-8</td><td>Level 3-4</td><td>60s holds, introduce active hangs</td><td>4 x 30-60s</td></tr>
              <tr><td>9-10</td><td>Level 4-5</td><td>Active hangs, introduce weight</td><td>3-4 x 20-30s</td></tr>
              <tr><td>11-12</td><td>Level 5-6</td><td>Weighted hangs, introduce one-arm if you meet its prerequisite</td><td>3 x 15-30s</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
        </div>

        <p>This plan assumes you start from zero. Skip ahead to the level that matches your current hold if you already have a base. Level 6 usually needs more grip training beyond these 12 weeks.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/training-programs/">training programs section</a> has 4, 8 and 12-week planning frameworks with review points.</p>

